---
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";

const useCases = [
  "Before buying or selling a car",
  "When insuring a vehicle",
  "After an accident for claim compensation",
  "For customs clearance and import duty",
  "In court matters such as succession or divorce cases",
  "To assess depreciation or fleet value"
];

const process = [
  {
    title: "1. Vehicle inspection",
    points: [
      "Overall body condition, paintwork, dents, scratches, and rust",
      "Tyre wear, alignment, lights, brakes, wipers, and other roadworthiness features",
      "Interior condition, dashboard condition, seats, and odometer reading",
      "Mechanical state including engine performance, suspension, and transmission",
      "Verification of chassis number and engine number"
    ]
  },
  {
    title: "2. Documentation review",
    points: [
      "Logbook details, registration, and ownership records",
      "Service and maintenance history",
      "Import records for foreign-used vehicles",
      "Accident history where available",
      "Previous valuation or assessment reports"
    ]
  },
  {
    title: "3. Market analysis",
    points: [
      "Current market prices for similar vehicles in Kenya",
      "Year of manufacture, age, and depreciation profile",
      "Mileage and general condition compared with similar units",
      "Demand trends for the model in the local market",
      "Availability of spare parts and any common model-specific issues"
    ]
  },
  {
    title: "4. Depreciation calculation",
    points: [
      "Vehicles commonly lose about 15% to 20% in the first year",
      "Later annual depreciation often falls around 10% to 15%, depending on condition",
      "Additional deductions can apply for accident damage, poor maintenance, or risky modifications",
      "Well-maintained vehicles with service records usually retain stronger value"
    ]
  },
  {
    title: "5. Final report preparation",
    points: [
      "Vehicle description and inspection photos",
      "Assessed current market value",
      "Pre-accident value where needed",
      "Depreciation analysis and comments on roadworthiness",
      "Official stamp and signature from the certified valuer"
    ]
  }
];
---

<MainLayout
  title="How Vehicle Valuation Is Done in Kenya | Unitech Accident Assessor"
  description="Learn how vehicle valuation is done in Kenya, from inspection and documentation review to market analysis, depreciation, and final reporting."
  pageTitle="How Vehicle Valuation Is Done in Kenya"
>
  <main class="page-main">
    <section class="content-section">
      <div class="content-grid">
        <article class="article-card">
          <p class="article-lead">
            Whether you are buying, selling, insuring, or importing a car, knowing its true market
            value is essential. In Kenya, vehicle valuation is a structured process that combines
            inspection, records review, depreciation analysis, and current market evidence.
          </p>

          <h2>Who performs vehicle valuations in Kenya?</h2>
          <p>
            Vehicle valuations are typically carried out by licensed motor vehicle valuers,
            certified vehicle assessors, and registered valuation companies such as Unitech. Their
            reports are often relied on by banks, insurers, customs authorities, legal teams, and
            private vehicle owners.
          </p>
          <p>
            In practice, these reports are especially important because they help create a fair and
            defensible basis for underwriting, financing, resale decisions, and dispute resolution.
          </p>

          <h2>When do you need a valuation?</h2>
          <ul class="content-list">
            {useCases.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
          </ul>

          <h2>The valuation process step by step</h2>
          <div class="article-steps">
            {process.map((step) => (
              <section class="step-card">
                <h3>{step.title}</h3>
                <ul class="content-list">
                  {step.points.map((point) => <li>{point}</li>)}
                </ul>
              </section>
            ))}
          </div>

          <h2>How much does valuation cost in Kenya?</h2>
          <p>
            For many private vehicles, valuation fees commonly fall between KES 3,000 and
            KES 10,000. Commercial vehicles, imported units, or more detailed assignments may cost
            more because they require additional inspection time and market work.
          </p>

          <h2>Why this process matters</h2>
          <p>
            A certified valuation protects you from underinsurance, poor buying decisions, and weak
            negotiation positions. It also gives insurers, courts, and financiers a professional
            document they can rely on when determining compensation or asset value.
          </p>
        </article>

        <aside class="sidebar-card">
          <p class="eyebrow">At a glance</p>
          <h2>Inspection, market evidence, and a signed report.</h2>
          <p>
            A proper Kenya vehicle valuation is not guesswork. It is a documented process built on
            the condition of the car and what similar vehicles are actually worth in the market.
          </p>
          <p class="eyebrow">Need help?</p>
          <a class="button button--primary" href="https://wa.me/254755668653" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
            WhatsApp Unitech
          </a>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</MainLayout>
